# Every text file is checked out with LF on every OS, whatever the contributor's core.autocrlf is (row 109). A Windows checkout with
# CRLF broke what reads exact bytes: the vendored public suffix list failed its sha256 (so every off-site verdict read "unknown site"
# on a Windows clone), digest-pinned fixtures, ledger fragments, and the node tests that extract functions out of app.js. Files
# committed with CRLF stay as committed (text=auto leaves a CRLF index copy alone). FIRST, so the rules below override it.
* text=auto eol=lf

docs/ledger/shipped.csv merge=union

# Windows scripts must keep CRLF endings whatever the contributor's core.autocrlf is:
# a .cmd checked out with bare LF can misparse multi-line blocks under cmd.exe.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
